    {
      "heading": "Long vowels and double consonants",
      "text": [
        "Long vowels count as two beats. In hiragana, add a vowel kana: おかあさん (okaasan), おにいさん, すうがく. Long e is usually written えい and long o usually おう: せんせい [seɴseː], ありがとう [aɾiɡatoː]. In katakana, use the bar ー: コーヒー [koːçiː].",
        "A small っ (or ッ) doubles the next consonant — you hold it for a beat before releasing: きって (kitte, stamp) vs きて (kite, come). In IPA it shows as a double letter: [kitte].",
        "ん is a beat of its own. It sounds like [m] before p, b, m (さんぽ [sampo]), [n] before t, d, n (ほんと [honto]), [ŋ] before k, g (ぎんこう [ɡiŋkoː]), and [ɴ] at the end of a word (ほん [hoɴ])."
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Reading the IPA in this app",
      "text": [
        "Pitch accent: Tokyo Japanese marks words with pitch, not stress. ꜜ shows where the pitch drops: はし [haꜜɕi] is chopsticks (high-low), はし [haɕiꜜ] is bridge (low-high, dropping on the next word). A word with no ꜜ is flat — it never drops: はな [hana], nose.",
        "Devoiced vowels: i and u between voiceless consonants, or at the end after one, are whispered or nearly silent. The small circle shows this: です [desɯ̥] sounds like \"dess\", した [ɕi̥ta] like \"shta\".",
        "Some letters to note: [ɯ] is an unrounded u — like \"oo\" with relaxed lips; [ɾ] is a light tap between r, l and d; [ɸ] is f made with both lips; [ɕ] and [tɕ] are softer than English sh and ch."
      ]
    }
